      "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\n**Root Cause:**\n`AtomWallet` operates as an ERC-4337 Smart Contract Wallet (SCW) intended to provide Account Abstraction (AA) features. The authorization mechanism for ERC-4337 `UserOperations` is handled in the `_validateSignature` function. \n\nCurrently, `_validateSignature` rigidly assumes that the `owner` of the `AtomWallet` is an Externally Owned Account (EOA) possessing a private key. It exclusively utilizes `ECDSA.tryRecover()` to derive the signer's address and directly compares it against `owner()`:\n\n```solidity\n// File: src/protocol/wallet/AtomWallet.sol:L285-L311\n    function _validateSignature(\n        PackedUserOperation calldata userOp,\n        bytes32 userOpHash\n    )\n        internal\n        virtual\n        override\n        returns (uint256 validationData)\n    {\n        (uint48 validUntil, uint48 validAfter, bytes memory signature) =\n            _extractValidUntilAndValidAfterFromSignature(userOp.signature);\n\n        bytes32 hash = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"\\x19Ethereum Signed Message:\\n32\", userOpHash));\n\n        // @audit - Exclusively uses ECDSA recovery. Cannot recover contract addresses.\n        (address recovered, ECDSA.RecoverError recoverError, bytes32 errorArg) = ECDSA.tryRecover(hash, signature);\n\n        if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureLength) {\n            revert AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureLength(uint256(errorArg));\n        } else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignatureS) {\n            revert AtomWallet_InvalidSignatureS(errorArg);\n        } else if (recoverError == ECDSA.RecoverError.InvalidSignature) {\n            return _packValidationData(true, validUntil, validAfter);\n        }\n\n        // @audit - Fails permanently if owner() is a Smart Contract (e.g., Multisig)\n        bool sigFailed = recovered != owner();\n        return _packValidationData(sigFailed, validUntil, validAfter);\n    }\n```\n\nBecause smart contracts (such as a Gnosis Safe, a DAO Treasury, or even the protocol's default `AtomWarden`) do not possess private keys, they cannot generate standard `r, s, v` ECDSA signatures. Consequently, `ecrecover` will either fail or recover an unrelated EOA address, ensuring that `recovered != owner()` always evaluates to `true`.\n\n**Impact:**\n1. **Bricking of Account Abstraction Features**: In the Intuition protocol, high-value `Atoms` representing institutions, DAOs, or community nodes will inherently be controlled via Multi-Signature wallets (e.g., Safe) to ensure security. Once ownership is transferred to a multisig, the `AtomWallet` loses all AA capabilities. The DAO will be permanently unable to route transactions through the `EntryPoint`, meaning they cannot utilize Paymasters for gas sponsorship, batch operations, or automated claim executions.\n2. **Default State Incompatibility**: Newly minted `AtomWallets` are by default owned by the `AtomWarden` (which is a smart contract). This implies that in its initial unclaimed state, the wallet inherently cannot interact with the ERC-4337 pipeline.\n\n\n\n## Recommended mitigation steps\n\nIntroduce **ERC-1271** (`isValidSignature`) support within the `_validateSignature` function. \n\nAdditionally, the rigid length check in `_extractValidUntilAndValidAfterFromSignature` (restricting signatures to 65 or 77 bytes) must be relaxed when the owner is a contract, as multisig signatures (e.g., Safe) are often concatenations of multiple signatures and will exceed 65 bytes.",
      "proofOfConcept": "## Proof of Concept\n\nAdd the following code to `tests/PoCCore.t.sol` and run it using `forge test --mt test_multisig_owner_validation_fails -vv`. \n\nThe test successfully demonstrates that even when a multisig returns a valid ERC-1271 approval, the `AtomWallet` unconditionally rejects the validation because it attempts an ECDSA recovery on a contract address.\n\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: BUSL-1.1\npragma solidity 0.8.29;\n\nimport { console2 } from \"forge-std/src/console2.sol\";\nimport { BaseTest } from \"./BaseTest.t.sol\";\nimport { AtomWallet } from \"src/protocol/wallet/AtomWallet.sol\";\nimport { PackedUserOperation } from \"@account-abstraction/interfaces/PackedUserOperation.sol\";\nimport { IERC1271 } from \"@openzeppelin/contracts/interfaces/IERC1271.sol\";\n\n// Mock a multisig wallet owner\ncontract MockMultisig is IERC1271 {\n    // Even if the contract approves the signature (ERC-1271 standard)\n    function isValidSignature(bytes32, bytes calldata) external pure returns (bytes4) {\n        return 0x1626ba7e; // IERC1271 magic value for success\n    }\n}\n\ncontract PoCCore is BaseTest {\n    AtomWallet wallet;\n    MockMultisig multisig;\n    bytes32 atomId;\n\n    function setUp() public override {\n        super.setUp();\n        \n        // 1. Create an Atom and deploy its associated wallet via the factory\n        atomId = createSimpleAtom(\"M-01 Test Multisig\", 1 ether, users.alice);\n        address walletAddr = protocol.atomWalletFactory.deployAtomWallet(atomId);\n        wallet = AtomWallet(payable(walletAddr));\n\n        // 2. Deploy the mock multisig contract\n        multisig = new MockMultisig();\n\n        // 3. Transfer wallet ownership to the multisig contract\n        // Initial owner is ATOM_WARDEN (set in BaseTest)\n        vm.startPrank(ATOM_WARDEN);\n        wallet.transferOwnership(address(multisig));\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Multisig contract accepts ownership\n        vm.startPrank(address(multisig));\n        wallet.acceptOwnership(); \n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // Verify ownership has been successfully transferred\n        assertEq(wallet.owner(), address(multisig));\n    }\n\n    /**\n     * @notice M-01 PoC: Demonstrates permanent ERC-4337 signature validation failure when the owner is a multisig contract\n     */\n    function test_multisig_owner_validation_fails() public {\n        // 4. Construct a UserOperation\n        // Note: signature must be exactly 65 bytes to bypass the hardcoded length check in AtomWallet\n        bytes memory dummySignature = new bytes(65); \n\n        PackedUserOperation memory userOp = PackedUserOperation({\n            sender: address(wallet),\n            nonce: 0,\n            initCode: \"\",\n            callData: abi.encodeWithSelector(AtomWallet.execute.selector, address(0x123), 0, \"\"),\n            accountGasLimits: bytes32(0),\n            preVerificationGas: 0,\n            gasFees: bytes32(0),\n            paymasterAndData: \"\",\n            signature: dummySignature \n        });\n\n        // Generate a fake UserOpHash\n        bytes32 userOpHash = keccak256(\"fakeUserOpHash\");\n\n        // 5. Simulate EntryPoint calling the validation function (ENTRY_POINT address is defined in BaseTest)\n        vm.prank(ENTRY_POINT);\n        uint256 validationData = wallet.validateUserOp(userOp, userOpHash, 0);\n\n        // 6. Parse validation result\n        // According to ERC-4337, the lowest bit of validationData is the sigFailed flag.\n        // sigFailed == 0 means success, sigFailed == 1 means validation failed.\n        uint256 sigFailed = validationData & 0x1;\n\n        console2.log(\"--- M-01 PoC Result ---\");\n        console2.log(\"Atom Wallet Owner (Multisig):\", wallet.owner());\n        console2.log(\"Is Signature Failed (1 = Yes):\", sigFailed);\n\n        // Assert validation failure (sigFailed == 1)\n        // Reason: AtomWallet._validateSignature enforces ecrecover,\n        // but ecrecover can never recover the address of a smart contract (MockMultisig),\n        // so sigFailed will invariably be 1.\n        assertEq(sigFailed, 1, \"VULNERABILITY CONFIRMED: Multisig owner cannot pass AA validation\");\n    }\n}\n```\n\n\n### console command and output\n```bash\nforge test --mt test_multisig_owner_validation_fails -vv\n\n[⠒] Compiling...\nNo files changed, compilation skipped\n\nRan 1 test for tests/PoCCore.t.sol:PoCCore\n[PASS] test_multisig_owner_validation_fails() (gas: 41756)\nLogs:\n  MultiVault implementation address:  0xF62849F9A0B5Bf2913b396098F7c7019b51A820a\n  MultiVault proxy address:  0x5991A2dF15A8F6A256D3Ec51E99254Cd3fb576A9\n  AtomWallet implementation deployed at: 0xc7183455a4C133Ae270771860664b6B7ec320bB1\n  AtomWalletBeacon deployed at: 0xa0Cb889707d426A7A386870A03bc70d1b0697598\n  AtomWalletFactory implementation address:  0x1d1499e622D69689cdf9004d05Ec547d650Ff211\n  AtomWalletFactory proxy address:  0xA4AD4f68d0b91CFD19687c881e50f3A00242828c\n  WrappedTrust address:  0x03A6a84cD762D9707A21605b548aaaB891562aAb\n  TrustBonding implementation address:  0xD6BbDE9174b1CdAa358d2Cf4D57D1a9F7178FBfF\n  TrustBonding proxy address:  0x15cF58144EF33af1e14b5208015d11F9143E27b9\n  SatelliteEmissionsController Implementation 0x212224D2F2d262cd093eE13240ca4873fcCBbA3C\n  SatelliteEmissionsController Proxy 0x2a07706473244BC757E10F2a9E86fB532828afe3\n  BondingCurveRegistry address:  0xD16d567549A2a2a2005aEACf7fB193851603dd70\n  LinearCurve address:  0x3381cD18e2Fb4dB236BF0525938AB6E43Db0440f\n  OffsetProgressiveCurve address:  0x1aF7f588A501EA2B5bB3feeFA744892aA2CF00e6\n  ProgressiveCurve address:  0x756e0562323ADcDA4430d6cb456d9151f605290B\n  Added LinearCurve to registry with ID: 1\n  Added OffsetProgressiveCurve to registry with ID: 2\n  Added ProgressiveCurve to registry with ID: 3\n  --- M-01 PoC Result ---\n  Atom Wallet Owner (Multisig): 0xC8501479803c58592eF3Be0beABBEE22e3377C08\n  Is Signature Failed (1 =